WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.424
Picture a café at lunchtime.

2
00:00:04.424 --> 00:00:07.380
You tap your card, and the screen says Approved.

3
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.581
Behind that word, five computers saved your payment.

4
00:00:10.581 --> 00:00:15.015
One of them, the leader, takes each payment and copies it to the others.

5
00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:17.914
Now the leader's network cable comes loose.

6
00:00:17.914 --> 00:00:20.087
How do the other four even notice?

7
00:00:20.087 --> 00:00:24.305
And how do they make sure exactly one of them takes over, not two?

8
00:00:24.305 --> 00:00:25.519
Hold that question.

9
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:27.953
Think of a club with a minutes book.

10
00:00:27.953 --> 00:00:31.159
Each period of office has one chair, chosen by vote.

11
00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:34.735
If the chair stops turning up, a member calls an election.

12
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:45.502
Our five computers work the same way: the chair is the leader, the period of office is a term, numbered 1, 2, 3, and the minutes book is the log of payments.

13
00:00:45.502 --> 00:00:47.393
Everyone else is a follower.

14
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.239
Watch it from the start, in the lab's run.

15
00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:51.558
Nobody is leader yet.

16
00:00:51.558 --> 00:00:54.071
Every server is a follower with a timer.

17
00:00:54.071 --> 00:00:58.720
If the timer runs out before it hears from a leader, it calls an election.

18
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.470
n4's timer runs out first, at 186 milliseconds.

19
00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:09.687
It moves to term 1, becomes a candidate, votes for itself, and asks the others for their votes.

20
00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:20.011
Each server gives at most one vote per term, to the first candidate that asks, and writes it to disk before answering.

21
00:01:20.011 --> 00:01:22.944
n1 votes at 187, n2 and n3 at 190.

22
00:01:22.944 --> 00:01:27.688
Three of five is a majority, so at 192, n4 wins term 1.

23
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:35.594
It sends a heartbeat at once, a small I'm still here message, and keeps sending one every 50 milliseconds.

24
00:01:35.594 --> 00:01:37.790
Each one restarts every timer.

25
00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.085
n5's vote arrives at 194 and changes nothing.

26
00:01:41.085 --> 00:01:43.135
The chair is already seated.

27
00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:45.616
Now n4's cable comes loose.

28
00:01:45.616 --> 00:01:49.364
The heartbeats stop, and the timers run down.

29
00:01:49.364 --> 00:01:51.364
Say n2's runs out first.

30
00:01:51.364 --> 00:01:55.529
It moves to term 2, and n1, n3 and n5 vote for it.

31
00:01:55.529 --> 00:01:56.861
n2 leads term 2.

32
00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:58.844
Then the cable goes back in.

33
00:01:58.844 --> 00:02:02.402
n4 still thinks it leads term 1, and sends a heartbeat.

34
00:02:02.402 --> 00:02:06.995
But every message carries the sender's term, and the reply says term 2.

35
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:15.208
A server that sees a higher term takes that number and becomes a follower, and a message with a lower term is refused.

36
00:02:15.208 --> 00:02:20.163
So n4 steps down at the first reply, and nobody acts on its old messages.

37
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.712
This method is Raft.

38
00:02:21.712 --> 00:02:29.845
The roles are follower, candidate and leader, the vote request is called RequestVote, and the timer is the election timeout.

39
00:02:29.845 --> 00:02:37.060
Two leaders in one term would need two majorities with no server in common, and five servers can't form those.

40
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.717
So does the timer matter much?

41
00:02:39.717 --> 00:02:47.772
Restart all five, but make every timeout exactly 150 milliseconds, and every message take exactly 5.

42
00:02:47.772 --> 00:02:48.497
Who wins?

43
00:02:48.497 --> 00:02:49.785
Pause and guess.

44
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:53.496
Nobody.

45
00:02:53.496 --> 00:02:56.472
All five time out in the same millisecond.

46
00:02:56.472 --> 00:03:02.709
Each votes for itself and refuses the other four, because it already voted in that term.

47
00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:03.843
Twenty refusals.

48
00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:09.149
150 milliseconds later, the same thing again, one term higher.

49
00:03:09.149 --> 00:03:14.265
After 10 seconds: 330 elections, term 66, and still no leader.

50
00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:19.238
A club where everyone nominates themselves at the same second, forever.

51
00:03:19.238 --> 00:03:20.514
The rules are fine.

52
00:03:20.514 --> 00:03:22.060
The tie is the problem.

53
00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:29.381
The fix is almost silly: draw each timeout at random, from 150 to 300 milliseconds in Raft's paper.

54
00:03:29.381 --> 00:03:34.933
One server usually wakes well before the others, and collects its votes first.

55
00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:37.909
The paper measured it on five servers.

56
00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:43.607
With no randomness, split votes left them leaderless for more than 10 seconds.

57
00:03:43.607 --> 00:03:49.524
With just 5 milliseconds of randomness, the median wait fell to 287 milliseconds.

58
00:03:49.524 --> 00:03:52.739
With 50, the worst of 1,000 trials took 513.

59
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.291
So why not make the timeout tiny?

60
00:03:55.291 --> 00:04:00.429
One round of messages, disk writes included, takes 0.5 to 20 milliseconds.

61
00:04:00.429 --> 00:04:06.887
The timeout must be many of those, or followers unseat a healthy leader that was merely slow.

62
00:04:07.067 --> 00:04:10.354
So it sits between 10 and 500 milliseconds.

63
00:04:10.354 --> 00:04:17.235
etcd, a small database built on Raft, waits 1,000 milliseconds before calling an election.

64
00:04:17.433 --> 00:04:19.504
And here the club picture breaks.

65
00:04:19.504 --> 00:04:21.889
Members in a room can see who's there.

66
00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:28.227
A server can't tell a dead leader from a slow network, so every rule runs on timers and term numbers.

67
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:34.697
So the moral: one leader per numbered term, won by a majority, one vote per server per term.

68
00:04:34.697 --> 00:04:38.604
Random timers break ties, and the higher term always wins.

69
00:04:38.867 --> 00:04:40.087
One quick check.

70
00:04:40.087 --> 00:04:42.680
Could two servers both win term 1?

71
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:43.900
Pause and think.

72
00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:47.383
No.

73
00:04:47.383 --> 00:04:51.351
Each winner needs three votes, so two winners need six.

74
00:04:51.351 --> 00:04:54.525
Five servers have only five votes in term 1.
